This file is an alphabetical listing of the Lisp files contained in this
package, with explanations and notes on various topics.
If you are looking for something to give you a general idea of whether
or not any of this is of use, try the files CHG, SQ, TCH, WMX, VBC, LYNX
or XING.

Note for 2nd release: The original RockLisp files are dated 03/15/91, newly
added and updated ones retain the dates of their creation or revision; also
I have marked them in this file with this clever tag: (New).  My two
favourites are SPRAY.LSP and SPYR.LSP, but if you need something a bit more
practical try BLAM.LSP or AT.LSP and ACH.LSP which are simple but very
useful.  Your best bet, though, is just to browse through this file and see
if anything strikes your fancy:



ABC.LSP
Puts text entities in vertical alphabetical order.  The text strings are
written to a file which is alphabetized using the Dos Sort command, then
the text is placed back into the entities starting with the one with the
highest Y coordinate and working down.  The Sort command has several
options which could be used to alter this routine in a variety of ways.

ACH.LSP (New)
Input a text string, what you would like it changed to, and select an
attribute.  This routine will change the first occurrence of the old string
in the attribute to the new string.  If you run it again you will be offered
your previous two strings as defaults, which is handy if the attribute has
several copies of the string in it or if you want to make the same changes
on more than one attribute.  Basically similar to Chgtext.lsp (which comes
with AutoCAD so you should know how it works).

ALIGN.LSP
Convert text to aligned text: select the text string, pick two endpoints.
If you happen to know what aligned text is for then this may be of use.

AT.LSP (New)
Replaces the text string displayed by an attribute without going through the
rather convoluted sequence of inputs demanded by Attedit.  Also offers the
new text string as the default the next time it is run, so you can make a
number of attributes say the same thing.  Makes editing blocks so much
easier that it may even stop the uninitiated from exploding them in
frustration.  See also ACH.LSP

ATTNAME.LSP
Changes an attribute to the drawing name, without path.  Saves a little
time and maybe ameliorates slightly the situation described in the following
section.  Also an example of how a lisp routine can avoid needless complexity
by using a built-in Autocad command.  See also QUONSET.LSP.

ATTX.LSP
Text replacer - works on text and the attributes left after a block has been
exploded.  This may seem like a useless ability; however there are a number of
misguided souls who insist on using attributes instead of text in title blocks.
This prevents them from being moved around, which is a good idea in theory,
but it also makes them difficult to adjust if the title does not coincide
with the preconceptions of the designer regarding text layout, length, etc.,
and difficult to edit if one is not generally familiar with attributes.
Inevitably someone will take the easy way out and explode the block, and
one never knows whether one is dealing with attributes, exploded attributes,
or real text.  This bit of lisp is a brief attempt to deal with the problem.
It may of course be incorporated into any routine which deals with supposed
text entities if the frequency of weird title blocks seems to so dictate.

AXE.LSP
Cartesian text editor.  This allows one to add text to the beginning or end
of an existing text entity, or remove the first or last word.  Choosing
among these options is done by picking points respectively to the right or
left and above or below the point by which the entity was selected.  Those
who hear this described are act to dismiss it as the work of an individual
not strongly anchored in reality; this is quite possibly true but Axe is also
a very useful command.  The principle of choosing between alternatives with
the digitizer rather than the keyboard is also one worth keeping in mind.
See also Pid.lsp.

BATCH.LSP (New)
A simple batch and script file writer.  This was designed to be used
whenever you want to apply a series of similar commands to a group of files
or other named entities.  Place the file names or other variables in a text
file (the default name is Varia.ble, but you can input a different one) and
the series of instructions or commands in another (default Template.txt) and
run BATCH.  The program creates a new file (default Fruit.bat) and copies
the template file into it, substituting the first variable name for each
question mark (?) that it finds, and repeats this process with successive
variables until it runs out of them.  After that you can of course manually
edit the file to put finishing touches on it and to check for errors.
The ability to specify template and variable file names allows you to keep a
variety of stock ones on hand and combine them as dictated by circumstances.
 Notes:  1.  Bear in mind that this can be used to construct batch files
             having nothing whatsoever to do with AutoCAD.  (It could
             also be used to write rather repetitive poetry...)
         2.  If you wish to put a blank line in your template file put
             a space on it or BATCH will ignore it.
         3.  The Lisp routine DIRT.LSP can be used to construct variable
             files from directory listings.
         4.  See also BLOCKED.LSP, CLEANER.LSP, PLDT.LSP.

BEEP.LSP (New)
Makes a beep.  Designed to notify the operator that a script or batch file
is finished.  Run it several times if you are unusually inattentive.

BLAM.LSP (New)
Explodes a block and replaces all of its attributes with text, deleting any
which consist solely of " " or "".  You are then free to edit them as your
heart desires, which despite the perverse dreams of block addicts is what
CAD is all about.  Great for title blocks (though I could probably be shot
for such a treasonous thought) and for freeing non-attribute entities trapped
like flies in amber.

BLCHG.LSP
Block changer.  Inserts a specified new block at each insertion of a
specified old one, matches rotation and X and Y scales on an individual
basis, and erases the old blocks.

BLG.LSP (New)
While amusing myself using Chgx to alter the bulge factors in segments of an
ellipse I decided to write a brief routine which steps through an entire
polyline and resets all of them to the same value.  In retrospect it is
quite similar to several available revision cloud makers but it has one
added ability: you can specify the bulge factor.  By making a thirty or
forty sided polygon (with the polygon command, in case you had forgotten)
and changing the bulge to say thirty five you can produce some very
spirograph-like entities; also squares, pentagons and so-on with a bulge of
six, minus six or something in that range produce rather interesting
geometric patterns.  Or you can use it to make clouds.

BLOCKED.LSP (New)
Finds every insertion of a specific block and changes a specific attribute
to a new text string.  The block name, attribute name, and text are prompted
for rather than (as in PLDT.LSP) built in, making this ideal for use in
batch files, which is what it was designed for.
Text is easier to manipulate than attributes and thus in my opinion is
superior for uses not involving the export of data from drawing files, but
in cases where it is liable to be changed in a fairly consistent manner in a
number of drawings (and especially where positioning varies) this routine
makes wholesale alterations with a script file reasonably painless , thus
justifying the use of the irritating things (attributes, not script files).

BOO.LSP
A variation of BOOM.  (See below.)  The same command, but does a Zoom
Extents using the same mechanism as ZE.LSP.  If you feel silly typing Boo
and can think of a better alternative, change it.  Booe, Boa...?

BOOM.LSP
The name derives from BOx and zoOM, in case you were wondering.  Zooms
Previous and draws a box around the view from which the zoom was performed.
(The box is erased by a Redraw.)  If you ever have to edit a lot of similar
details and have trouble remembering where exactly you were, this routine
is invaluable. It's also a good thing to have if you're not very interested
in what you're doing.

BOX.LSP
A "draw a box by picking the two opposite corners" routine.  Everyone has
one of these, but none of them seem to use the Getcorner function to
display all four sides of the box before it is drawn.  This one does.

BOXX.LSP
A debugging aid for LYNX.  Draws a box exactly enclosing one or more
selected lines.  (see LYNX.LSP)

CENTRE.LSP
Convert text to centre justified text.  Hit Return to use the original
insertion, pick two points and the text will be placed at a point halfway
between them, or pick a point and Return and the text will be centred on
that point.  If you can't be bothered to read all this then just try it out.
See also Align.lsp, Left.lsp, Fit.lsp, Middle.lsp and Right.lsp.

CHEC.LSP
Cyclic recursion checker.  This doesn't actually do anything, but it gives
the impression that it is, and something very technical at that.  A trial
run will prove more informative than an attempted explanation.
Note: 1.  This routine requires the file Chec.dwg (which is included).
      2.  If you use fixed height text with this it should be three
          units high.

CHG.LSP
A simple and yet inspired program.  Pick an entity, Chg returns its database
listing, tell it which association list to change and it prompts for the
correct type of input, gives the modified data back to AutoCAD, and tells
whether or not the new data was accepted and the entity changed. 
This is simple enough, but it gives the user a much greater ability to
change existing entities than the standard edit commands allow; also it
is useful for testing in advance what that new routine you're writing will
really do to an entity, and it lets you play with entity data and find out
what various bits of the association list really control.  It is also
a good way to generate ideas for new lisp routines.
Cautionary notes:  1. Sometimes (rarely) AutoCAD will accept the modified
                      association list, but will not actually change the
                      entity - in effect the list snaps back to the original
                      value as soon as the command ends.  If the program says
                      that the modifications were OK but nothing happens, use
                      Chg or GET.LSP to see if anything really changed.
                   2. If you use Chg to alter the (8 . "layername")
                      association list and the layer you name does not 
                      exist, it will be created.  Be careful.

CHGX.LSP
This is a slightly more elaborate version of CHG.LSP.  It allows one to step
through subentities one at a time and modify a selected one.  It is more
powerful than CHG, but a little less elegant to use, if you care about that
sort of thing.

CLEANER.LSP (New)
A brief implementation of the ability of the Wblock command to block an
entire drawing, thus deleting all unreferenced blocks, layers, styles, etc.
This usually reduces file size significantly, as an extreme case I have seen
600k files reduced to 95k: the operator had imported five 100k files while
searching for a single block, then used the resulting drawing as a
prototype.  The Purge command can of course take care of individual
instances, but CLEANER can be run at any time in the editing session and
also lends itself to use with batch files, which even if your drawings are
fairly lean can free a startling amount of memory in short order.
The skeptical may point out that you don't need a Lisp routine to Wblock
a whole drawing.  True, but this is fast, easy to remember, and points out
the possibility to those who may not have known of it.
A note on batch file use: because CLEANER quits the drawing while still in
AutoLISP, control is not handed back to the batch file and the process
terminates.  This can be avoided by deleting the line (COMMAND "QUIT" "Y")
and putting the QUIT Y command in the batch file itself.  If you use END
instead of QUIT then you will overwrite the purged version of the drawing
created by WBLOCK and nothing will be changed.
This routine was suggested and the method explained by MI in the course of a
phone call.  The result is invaluable but I no longer recommend programming
on the phone as a relaxing pastime.

CLK.LSP
Draws a clock, set to the correct time.  I have used a circle as the face,
but a line has been included in the file to insert a block called "clk"
if you want to draw one.  (You will have to move the semicolon from the
beginning of the "insert" line to the beginning of the "circle" line)
People always ask on seeing this if the clock continues to keep time once
it is inserted.  It doesn't.  But feel free, if you can think of a reason.

CT.LSP
Inserts multiple lines of text.  You supply a letter, text to put before and
after, spacing, height (if the current text style is not that wretched fixed
height substance), number of letters by which to increment the specified one,
etc.  If the routine runs out of alphabet it starts over, placing an A in
front of the variable letter.  The next time the leading A is incremented to
B, and so on, for a total possible of 676 lines.
See also NT.LSP which does the same thing with numbers (but can handle more
than 676 lines).

CURL.LSP
Moves selected entities to the current layer.  I have been informed that
this is of no use whatsoever, here it is anyway.

DAB.LSP
Line and block inserter.  Constructs a line with breaks at intervals into
which a block is inserted.  One can specify block scale, size of space to
leave for the block, line segment length, etc.  There are other routines
available which can do this, this one is faster than the ones I have seen
and dispenses with the thirty or forty required inputs.
See also LAC.LSP, LAC1.LSP and LACC.LSP.

DEROT.LSP
Derotates blocks.  If you really can't figure out what that means, try
it out.  Revision note: Derot will now also work with Text.  If you
want to derotate lines, use LYNX.LSP with ortho on.  Circles and points
you will have to handle on your own.

DIRT.LSP (New)
Writes a directory listing to a text file and edits the file leaving nothing
but an alphabetical list of file names without extensions.  Subsets of the
directory listing can be selected using Dos wild cards etc.  This program
was designed to create variable files for use with Batch.lsp.
A modification of Dirtx (see below).

DIRTX.LSP
Copies the specified directory listing to a file, optionally sorts
alphabetically, and optionally removes everything but the file name and
extension and replaces any spaces between them with a period.
I wrote this specifically to create the basic list of file names which I
edited into this text file.  It is probably not all that useful except as
a demonstration of AutoLisp's ability to issue commands to Dos and to
directly manipulate text files.  On the other hand, it's aesthetically
pleasing to run.

EARTH.LSP
Draws a circle centred on the current view and having the same diameter
as the Earth.  Immensely useful.  If you don't try it at least once
then you're not human.  (Assumes that your drawing units are millimetres,
but values for other units are provided in case you want to change this.)

ED.LSP
Several popular Lisp packages advertise something like: "Full function text
editing within Autocad."  After wondering for some time how this could be
done the following obvious solution came to me as I sat in a daze before
a foot-thick stack of scrawled blueprints: copy the text string to an
external text file, shell out, edit the file with a text editor, re-enter
Autocad, read the text file, and substitute the new string for the old
one in the text entity.  This does all of that automatically (except for
selecting the text string and deciding what you want it to say - there
seemed to be little call for a program which made arbitrary alterations to
a randomly selected text entity).
While it was my intention to make all of these routines ready to use with
no modification to them or the system, this one uses a special shell
command and a text editor.  Since I don't own the rights to a text editor
you will have to use your own.  (You could use Edlin if nothing else is
available, but then you could also wash your clothes by beating them on
a flat rock and forage for nuts and berries...)
The line: (command "XXX" "edtex") uses the shell command "XXX" to shell
out to a text editor.  You will have to add the line:
XXX,editor,200000,File to edit: ,4
to your Acad.pgp file, where "editor" is the name of the fastest loading
simple text editor available which produces pure ascii output.  (If your
text editor loads and runs too slowly this routine will be too irritating
to be useable.)
If you are not familiar with the shell command file Acad.pgp see
the file Sample.pgp which is included with this package.

EXPL.LSP
For those times when you get the irritating "Can't explode that block
because the X scale factor is 0.002 and the Y is 137" message.  This sets
the Y and Z scales equal to the X and explodes the block, which is not a
perfect solution but is probably as good as one can hope for until AutoCAD
allows different X and Y scales for objects other than blocks.  (Actually I
suppose one could write something to descale blocks, explode them, reblock
the individual entities, reinsert them using the previous scale factors and
then descale, explode and edit them on an individual basis.  Go ahead.  You
don't even have to credit me for the idea.)  Expl can also be used to window
an area and explode all of the blocks in it.

FIBL.LSP
Find blocks - prompts for a block name, then inserts a temporary X over
the insertion point of each occurrence.  This routine does not do a Zoom All
before placing the X's, since many people object to programs which force a
regen which may be either unnecessary or very time consuming, but it is a
good idea to have the entire drawing on screen before searching it.

FIND.LSP
An implementation of AutoLisp's Findfile function.  Finds a specified file 
and sets the ff variable which is used as the default for LD, XX, SCR.

FINDX.LSP
This is a variation of the above.  If the specified find is not found it
searches for the same name with alternate extensions from a list (which
can be edited) contained within the lisp file.

FINTX.LSP
Find text.  Similar to FIBL.LSP - input a text string, this puts an X at
the insertion point of any text entity which contains it.  The search is
case sensitive, I had considered making this optional but I believe that
a routine which requires answering too many prompts will likely not be
used: witness the plethora of Lisps available to copy text while rotating
it and changing the layer to one created on the spot.  Such things are
occasionally useful but by the time the need arises one has invariably
forgotten them.  (One can of course edit out unneeded options and set the
defaults to something useful.  You can draw your own conclusions if the
result exactly duplicates an existing AutoCAD command.)
If a different feature is really needed a second program is often a better
option.  If it isn't used often enough to rate a place on the digitizer
you can bury it (and the load command) in the third layer of the pull-down
menus.
Later note:  The problem of remembering the names and functions of lisp
routines is one which I should probably address, given the number of the
things included here.  As mentioned above, a command is of limited use if
you can't remember its name when you need it.  A couple of approaches
suggest themselves:
    1. Rename the routines you might actually use to something you can
       remember.
    2. Make a set of pull-down icon menus with a brief description in each
       of the lisp routine it will run.
    3. Draft a wall chart of all of your available lisps sorted by name
       or function or type of entity affected and nail it to the wall
       beside your monitor.  Make it reasonably attractive so that people
       will ask you for copies rather than shred it in your absence.
    4. Put little tiny descriptions on your digitizer.  Text is more
       readable than those irritating little pictures, anyway.  If you
       must use ideograms make some which have some relation to what they
       are supposed to indicate (not that the ones from the standard
       AutoCAD menu aren't as elegant as they are informative).
    5. Print out a copy of the file Briefndx.doc which I have thoughtfully
       included.

FIT.LSP
Converts text to fitted text: select text, pick two endpoints.  Uses the
original insertion as the default for one end (or both if you want text of
zero width).  See also Align.lsp, Left.lsp, Fit.lsp, Middle.lsp, Right.lsp
and Vb.lsp.

GET.LSP
Prints the data for a selected entity to the screen.  The default is the
entity after the last one selected, so a polyline or block can be stepped
through one part at a time.  (See CHG.LSP to actually edit entities in this
manner.)

HT.LSP
Text height changer.  Select one or more entities, change them to a chosen
height.  Also works on fixed height text, and will ignore non-text entities
so you can window an area and change only the text.  (Revised: now allows
picking an entity of the desired height.)  See also Wt.lsp which does the
same thing with text width scale factors.

HVB.LSP
Horizontal Venetian Blind.  Rearranges text so that it's all at the same
level vertically, without moving it horizontally.  (Yes, it should have been
called VVB.LSP, but the name was already taken.  You can rename it if you
really want.  Actually, unless you are in the position of having to routinely
enter commands from the keyboard, longer names generally seem to provide
a better clue to what the program actually does.)  Pick the text, specify
a point, and choose whether you want to align it by the text insertion point
or on the left end of the text line (or what would be the left end if it were
upright - this feature is intended for use with rotated text).  Unlike VB.LSP
etc., this merely moves text - the registration is not altered.

IHI.LSP
(This and the next two files are included as examples of different line
breaking block inserters.  The different methods used can be easily changed
or recombined to make routines appropriate to specific uses.)
Insert a circle on a line and break out the centre, using the trim command.
This was written for a purpose, although I was never told what that might
be.  Again, included mainly as an example.  Possibly "-0-0-0-.LSP" would
have been a more informative name, but most people rightly loathe file and
program names with dashes, underscores etc. in them.

IHI2.LSP
Insert a block on a line and chop out the centre.  Uses a slightly different
technique from the above, possibly more useful.  The width of the cut can
be adjusted fairly easily by modifying the line containing the Break command.

IHI3.LSP
Insert a circle, trim out the centre, erase the circle and insert a block
in its place.

INF.LSP
Prints (to the screen) various useful information about the drawing,
punctuation, wildlife, etc.

JACK.LSP (New)
Inserts a block at a scale equal to Dimscale.  Written so that drawings
which have been scaled can still be worked on without losing all sense
of proportion (if you thought to set dimscale to the scale factor).
See also SING.LSP.  (Thanks for the idea, Jack.)

L.LSP
This is not a program which does anything remarkable or unusual, but if you
aren't aware of the possibility of such a thing, it...they...that not good.
There are three sections:
       1. The error handler. This replaces AutoLisp's standard error handler
          with one which merely tells you the nature of the error - usually
          that you cancelled a lisp routine in the middle - rather than
          spitting out about five screens of text.  (If you are debugging
          Lisp routines you can remove this or temporarily disable it with
          a row of semicolons.)
       2. The Sysvar initializer.  Turns on the status line co-ordinate
          display, sets snap to a realistic value, sets the limits to
          the extents, turns off the ucs icon, etc.  Again, you can add
          more or remove any you don't need.
       3. Lisp loader.  Loads selected lisp routines without having to
          copy them into ACAD.LSP and then update them each time they
          are modified.
You can load this manually by typing (load "L") and then type L at the
"Command:" prompt to run it, or you can copy it into ACAD.LSP and then
put (DEFUN S::STARTUP () (C:L)) at the end which will run it automatically.
(The S::STARTUP function does not work with Autocad releases earlier than
nine, if you are using an older AutoCAD version you will have to type the
"L" in manually.  Actually, typing "L" is not all that strenuous and allows
one to save time by not loading the lisp routines when they aren't needed.)
None of this is particularly Earth-shaking, but it saves a little time and
trouble, and it is handy if you don't have access to the ACAD.LSP file and
want to use your own Lisps.  (Why anyone would encrypt ACAD.LSP is beyond
me, but the lust for secrecy sometimes outgrows the theoretical desire to
accomplish anything.)

LAC.LSP
Asks for two endpoints, then draws a series of line segments and circles
between them.  The line length is fixed at 10 and the circle diameter at 2,
which avoids having to mess with defaults and allows the program to run
rapidly, if those dimensions happen to be what you had in mind.
If you want to change the line length and circle diameter you can edit
the file (cautiously) or use Lacc: see below.

LAC1.LSP (New)
Converts existing lines to line and circle composites.  Sounds a bit like
overkill but is nice to have if you are working with two parties who have
conflicting ideas on linetype standards as I was when I wrote it.
Line length is 10, circle diameter 2, just like LAC.LSP, upon which it is
largely based.

LACC.LSP
Which allows you to specify the line length, circle diameter, gap between
the two, and endpoints, with a performance penalty that is pretty minimal
unless you are still using an XT.

LADD.LSP
Combines two lines of text.  Pick the base line, pick the second, this will
add the second one onto the end of the first; also it removes any excess
spaces from between the two (see also SPARE.LSP).  Designed for those
situations where WMX.LSP might be a bit tedious.

LC.LSP
Layer change with screen menu.  This one will only handle as many layers as
will fit on the screen menu.  You could probably devise a multi-page menu if
you are addicted to hundreds of layers...I could, too, but so far no suitable
incentive has put in an appearance.

LCH.LSP
Select objects, pick an object on the desired layer, move the former to
coincide in that respect with the latter.

LCS.LSP
Change text to lower case.  (See also UC.LSP - This one was going to be
called LC, but the name was already taken.)

LD.LSP
Lisp loader.  Saves trying to get the command right when you aren't awake yet,
and shares the ff default variable with XX, FIND, and SCR, in an attempt to
make repeated editing and reloading of files a little less tedious.  I use
these all the time.  (Looking back, this isn't that clear.  These four
programs all use the global variable ff to store the name of the last file
edited, so that when another one is used it will present the same file name
as the default.  They will also add the appropriate extension if none is
provided.  This is pretty simple, but it saves a lot of aggravation.)

LEFT.LSP
Convert text to left justified text; specify a new insertion point or by
default use the original one.  If you want to move left justified text using
the insertion as the base point this is quicker than the move command.
See also Align.lsp, Left.lsp, Fit.lsp, Middle.lsp, Right.lsp and Vbl.lsp

LIC.LSP (New)
An alternative to the Change command for those times when the wrong end
of the line you are editing snaps over to the new point.  LIC moves the
end closest to the pick point, not the one closest to the change point,
which in my opinion gives a greater degree of control.

LIX.LSP
Sets the limits to the extents.  Useful if you find the grid occupying
only a postage stamp sized area of your screen, if your Zoom All produces
mostly empty space, or if plotting to limits produces a handy wallet-sized
picture.  A batch file which runs through a number of drawings Zooming to
extents and then using MVA.LSP and LIX can cure a variety of irritations.

LSET.LSP
Pick an object and thus set the current layer.  Also tells you what layer
you were on and what layer you are on.  You could just pay attention to
the status line, but your mind might be having coffee or you just might
have my fondness for commands that tell you what you have just done as
opposed to what you think you have just done.

LT.LSP
Change linetype by object selection.  Select some entities, pick one of the
desired linetype, the previous ones will be changed to match.  The default
linetype (continuous) is not always explicitly contained in the entity
database, so this creature adds that entry to the entity data list if it
can't find it, and then modifies it as required.  All of the books on Lisp
which I have come across claim that this is impossible, so this may not work
with earlier releases (pre-10).  Then again, maybe I imagined the whole thing.

LTR.LSP
Accepts a file name.  If the file exists, writes the last line to the status
line.  It then writes all text typed in into the file, starting a new line
with each <Return>.  A Return with no input terminates the procedure, and the
filename is saved as the ff variable common to XX (and also LD, FIND and SCR)
so that you can pick up where you left off.  This might be useful for writing
batch files or something, but mostly it was designed to allow one to write
letters while appearing to be working in AutoCAD.

LYNX.LSP (Revised)
(Update note: After using Lynx in a number of drawings I became aware of
two shortcomings in its design:
    1. It puts the line on snap even though you may have liked it the way
       it was.  This is a major irritation if you change snap settings often.
    2. The resulting line is placed on the current layer regardless of which
       layer the original ones occupied.
I have modified Lynx so that it makes the combined line match the first one
selected with respect to colour, linetype and layer, and have removed and
enlarged the snap adjuster into a stand-alone routine: Snake.lsp.
The original version of Lynx is still included under the name Slynx.)

A rather elaborate program (or should I use the more stylish "application"?)
which combines a number of line segments into one.  This was originally
intended to deal with lines which overlay each other (assume that I make
some comment here about people who do that sort of thing) and evolved into
a device for repairing lines which had been mistakenly cut, connecting pieces
of logic diagrams, etc.
Since in order to be able to handle a variety of possible situations this
program has to make certain assumptions about what is desired (and also
because you might want to modify it) I will include a brief outline: Lynx
compares all endpoints of the lines it is shown and finds the greatest and
smallest X and Y value.  In effect it constructs a box around the lines.
(Should you wish to modify Lynx, the included Lisp routine Boxx actually
draws this box and can be used to see how well your modifications work.)
It also checks the angle of the lines to see if the majority of them are
angled to the right or the left or are horizontal or vertical, and determines
if snap and ortho are on.  Then Lynx draws a line from the upper left corner
of the box to the lower right corner, or from the lower left to upper right,
depending on the predominant angle of the selected lines.  If they lean right
and left in equal numbers (though I have no idea why you would in such a case
want to combine them) or if ortho is on the line is drawn down the middle of
the box in whichever direction is the longest.  It has, of course, erased the
original lines before doing all of this.
A couple of further points:
         1.  I didn't really understand how this thing worked until after I
             had it running, so it should (if you wish) be easier to modify
             than it was to write.
         2.  Lisp stands for (Yeah, Lost in...) List Processing.  Why then
             will the Min and Max functions not accept a list as input?
             Surely this is the only logical way of passing them information.
             Is this why one almost never comes across a routine which makes
             use of them?

MIDDLE.LSP
Convert text to middle justified text.  Fairly self explanatory but if it
really baffles you get an adult to take a look.  See also Align.lsp,
Left.lsp, Fit.lsp, Middle.lsp and Right.lsp.

MVA.LSP
Moves the lower left point of the drawing to 0,0.  If you have macros to
insert a block or edit text at a specific point, this might come in handy.
Like many of these, it will only be of use to those who share their drawings
with the careless or unbalanced.

NAMB.LSP (New)
Pick a block, this prints its name on the command line and at the bottom of
the screen menu.  You could List the block, hit pause, read the name, and
let the rest of the information scroll past, but this is simpler.
(If you remove the semicolons from in front of the three lines toward the
end of the file it will become a bit more emphatic.  Handy if you want to
irritate someone.)

NT.LSP
Incremented numbers with text.  See CT.  I have never used this, or even
seen a vague theoretical need for it, but my old buddy MI felt that it
would be better for me to spend several hours writing and debugging it
than for him to spend several minutes working without it.
It's interesting to watch, though.

NUM.LSP
Change existing text to sequential numbers given a start and increment.
This can also handle negatives and reals, should the urge strike you.
Created because it's generally easier to modify existing entities than to
manufacture a variety of new ones.  (Revised: now works even if Luprec is
set to 0, and saves the latest number as the default if used again.)

NUMTX.LSP
A variation of Num: allows a prefix and a suffix; otherwise similar.

ONE.LSP
Pick a text entity which contains only a number, this uniquely useful
routine prints the English equivalent on the command line.  (In case you
find this inexplicably difficult to understand, it would convert 1234 to
"one thousand two hundred and thirty four".)  I have not bothered to add a
routine to remove punctuation, spaces and letters from the string before
translating it, choosing instead to regard One as the core around which an
almost infinite variety of programs can be constructed.
As it stands, One can handle numbers of up to about thirty six digits.  If
you wish to use it with larger ones you will have to add the names for the
next few to the end of the list.  You might also wish to examine the ones
which I have included - the designations for anything over a million are
still not universally accepted, and I have not hesitated to take a stand
where convention fails.

OTX.LSP
Text at your spacing and angle.  Originally intended to get around AutoCAD's
inability to place successive lines of text at a user specified separation,
this routine now allows the operator to specify insertion, registration type,
line spacing, text height, text string angle, and the angle between the
insertion points of consecutive lines of text.  This may sound needlessly
complex, but it allows one to place text for example in horizontal lines
5mm apart in a vertical array, or at a 35 degree angle to the vertical
spaced 7mm apart proceeding to the right.  Running through it about twice
should leave the average user completely familiar with its capabilities
and yet still slightly in awe.
In case the plethora of prompts becomes tedious I have included a default
value for each criterion, which can be edited to one more suited to the
user given a little care and a text editor.  (Alternately you could build
them into your menu structure.)
For the sake of completeness it was felt neccessary to include a default
text string, and so that this should not pall too rapidly I have used my
random number generator with a list of phrases to add some variety (See
RAN.LSP).  My apologies to anyone who was a German army officer in an old
war movie.

PID.LSP (New)
Invented while working on the diagrams of the same name, this routine adds a
prefix or suffix (or both) to a text string.  If you miss or press Return or
have yet to define the two, you will be prompted to do so.  If you want to
get rid of a default a single space is interpreted as an empty string:
nothing is added or appended as the case may be.  This is quite a useful bit
of Lisp, the hardest part is remembering that it exists when it is needed.
See also Axe.lsp.

PKILL.LSP
A handy routine which removes all point entities from a drawing.  Places a
large (temporary) X over each point entity in the drawing, even if they are
invisible, then asks if you would like to delete them.
This suggested itself after one of many conversations with MI concerning
drawings which when zoomed to extents shrink down to postage stamp size in
one corner of the screen and continue to so do even after several
repetitions.  The evil one says he has had good success blocking the desired
areas and then importing the resulting block into an existing blank drawing.
However, he recently mentioned having found a drawing which was full of
points which had been set so as not to display (Pdmode = 1), but which
occupied a large and apparently empty area.  Since you can't get hold of
an invisible point by windowing it, you can either setvar pdmode to 0 and
regen, or use Pkill.
If you use points for something other than snap nodes (i.e. you are heavily
into pointilism) you might want to avoid this program.
Also see SDEL.LSP and TXKILL.LSP.

PL.LSP
Plot initiate utility.  Uses most recent defaults, and names the plot
file by combining the initials of the user (These are stored in the file
Name.txt which will be created if it is not found) with the last five
characters of the drawing name, or the letters after a backslash if one
is found before five letters are counted.  As written the routine places
the plot file in the directory D:\PLOT\, this can of course be changed.
If you change plot parameters with some frequency this will be of limited
use, but it provides a model for building lisp files which write and then
run script files.  It could presumably be expanded into a program which
prompts for plot size etc. and runs the plot while avoiding the more common
plotting errors, or copied and modified into several routines for various
sizes, scales, etc.  See Plonk.lsp for a further development of this idea.

PLANT.LSP (New)
This is the program you have been dreaming of: an automatic plant maker.
It could probably be refined to some extent, but the lack of any conceivable
use has stifled further R&D.  The plants produced tend to be rather
geometric, the larger ones - the ones with a higher number of iterations -
usually appear more plant-like but take longer to draw; three is a good
starting number.  A block named "Leaf" is inserted at the end of each branch,
one is provided so that the program won't crash when you run it without
reading the instructions; you can substitute a more elaborate block of your
own if you wish, a simple one (such as the one provided) will insert faster,
an important consideration for large plants and old computers.  Each plant
is different so you may have to draw a few to get one that you like.

PLDT.LSP
Change a selected attribute in a selected block to the date.  This was
written to automatically date-stamp drawings as part of a plot tracking
system, hence the name: PLot DaTe.  Unlike other methods which use a text
string placed at a standard point, this program will find the appropriate
block anywhere in the drawing, regardless of scale or placement, it will
also find and alter multiple copies.  (Strangely enough, I can actually
think of a use for multiple date-stamping.)
The block which will be altered must be called REVDATE and the attribute
ATTDATE unless, as usual, you want to edit the file.  Actually, it could
be edited into a variety of automatic block change programs.

PLONK.LSP (New)
Automatic plot file generator.  A further development of Pl.lsp, Plonk
extracts plot parameters from the block Qaplot (included) and writes a
script file which runs the plot command, changing the settings at the same
time.  This allows saving of plot settings in the drawing file: once they
are correct you don't have to go through the whole procedure by hand or
forget and plot a postage stamp, and a drawing can be used as a prototype
for another of the same size and scale and will still plot correctly.
The block is edited with DDATTE which allows the plot data to be examined
without running a plot and changed repeatedly and in an order other than
that dictated by the plot command.
Qaplot.dwg consists solely of invisible attributes, thus it does not mess up
the appearance of the drawing and can't be accidentally erased (Plonk allows
you to edit or erase it, or you could insert a visible entity in the block
drawing to use as a pick point).
This version of Plonk will write the plot to a file, if your plotter
requires a slightly different input sequence you may wish to edit Plonk or
modify Qaplot, the file is commented fairly thoroughly.

PNEUM.LSP (New)
Pneumatic line constructor.  Inserts block PNEU90 in vertical lines and
PNEU0 in horizontal ones, both on 12 unit spacing.  The idea is that the
sets of diagonal slashes commonly used to denote pneumatic lines for some
reason look wrong if the slashes placed on vertical lines are the same as
the slashes on the horizontal ones.  Rather than try to change the standard
form, I wrote this.  Both blocks are provided, or you can make your own.

PROP.LSP
Block reproportioner.  Sets the X, Y, and Z scales in a block to 1.  Can be
used with DEROT.LSP to restore blocks to their original state.  I suppose
you could just reinsert them, but one likes to have a special routine.
Anyway, this is less tedious.
Technical note: this routine reproportions the block in question by
modifying the head entity for the insertion.  This does not change the
attributes, though.  One could fairly easily modify the program to step
through the attribute subentity data and descale them individually but they
would still be out of position with respect to the rest of the block.  The
obvious answer is a routine which moves them individually to the correct
insertion point; I would have written one but have instead occupied my time
wondering whether anyone would actually want to descale a block with
attributes.
See also Props.lsp which for your convenience is the next entry.

PROPS.LSP (New)
Similar to Prop but allows you to either make all of the scales match either
the existing X, Y, or Z factor for each block selected or input another
number.

QUONSET.LSP (New)
Sets the attribute Quonset in the block Qname to the drawing name without
the path.  This was designed to be called by (S::STARTUP) so that drawings
would always bear the correct file name - a matter of some importance if you
are working with more than four drawings.  The block Qname is included; it
consists solely of the attribute Quonset. You can of course modify it or
make your own.
Note that the attribute is updated even if it is not on the screen, so you
can put it anywhere you want (or even use multiple copies) and you don't
have to do a Zoom Extents before running this routine.  Also see PLDT,
ATTNAME, TXMD, TXD, TXN, and TXTT.

RA.LSP
Redraw with timer.  Fairly useless, which is why I have included the
similar files ZP.LSP and ZA.LSP.  You will be surprised by how little time
a redraw really takes.  Maybe.

RAN.LSP
Random number generator.  This is the basis of SIX and of the varying text
string selector used in TCH and OTX.  There is not much random about
computers, so the microsecond at which the command was entered (or at
which it reached the actual program line involved) is used.  I can't
think of any practical applications, but one could probably write some
nice gambling programs or games to run in Autocad.

RIGHT.LSP
Convert text to right justified text.  The default insertion point is the
original or you can pick a new one - it's one of those decisions which the
program can't make for you.  See also Align.lsp, Left.lsp, Fit.lsp,
Middle.lsp, Right.lsp and Vbl.lsp

RT.LSP
Reaction time tester.  Also uses RAN in several places.  The figures used
for determining what is an acceptable reaction time are only estimates
based on a limited sample (myself) so don't draw too many conclusions
from your results.

RTALL.LSP
Reverse the order of letters in every text string in a drawing.  This
confuses some people, so listen: it doesn't mirror image them, it
reverses the order - "Hi gang" becomes "gnag iH".  Running it a second
time will restore the original state of affairs.  As suggested in the
file header, this is of no use (I seem to say that a lot) unless you
have already found another job.

RTX.LSP
Reverse one text string.  Same as the above, but you can give it a try
without doing the whole drawing.  The CAD equivalent of doodling.

SC.LSP
Style change with screen menu.  I'm quite fond of this one.  (I am told
that it will crash Lightning Zoom, so ST.LSP is provided which is similar
but won't.  On the other hand you might try it out once before assuming
the worst - I may have been misinformed by the thumbfingered.)
Select text strings, pick a style from the screen menu.  Any further
explanation would be superfluous.

SCR.LSP
Script loader.   Shares ff default with XX, LD, and Find.  Basically
completes the edit aid foursome.  Will add the .scr extension and run
as the default the last file edited with XX.

SDEL.LSP (New)
A departure from tradition: a program written for my own use.  This routine
lets you select items using the standard procedure and then prompts for a
type of entity - Line, Circle, Insert (Block), Text, etc., and erases from
the drawing all objects of that type that are in the selection set.  If you
want it can also delete every entity of a given type from the entire drawing.
Not the type of thing one uses every day, but (like dynamite) likely to come
in handy if you know it's there.

SHUX.LSP
Text vertical spacing adjuster.  Similar to VVB.LSP (and written because
the first person to whom I gave VVB was somewhat unappreciative...maybe even
outright critical).   Select text and pick the new points for the top of
the top line and the bottom of the bottom one, this routine will
space them all evenly between.  I can't see any use for this at all,
and I've seen it used.  Still, somebody likes it, so here it is.
(I was recently informed that a large percentage of my lisps deal with the
manipulation of text.  This is so: the largest amount of (and in my opinion
the most tedious) time spent working in Autocad is that spent creating and
editing text entities.  Also Lisp lends itself to text manipulation and yet
most users seem to confine themselves to three dimensional corinthian column
generators and other programs of not unlimited general usefulness.)
(Later note: The top point is the top of the upper case characters in the
top line - if there are only lower case ones there will be a gap.  You
could allow for this.)

SING.LSP (New)
A variation of XING, SING breaks the first line of two selected.  This was
suggested by Brian (whose last name escapes me), and while I initially
bridled at the implied criticism of XING, it is actually a good idea.  SING
can't handle more than one intersection at a time, but it:
  1.  Allows you to break either the vertical or horizontal line,
  2.  Breaks linetypes other than continuous with no danger of missing
      the line, and
  3.  Can be modified to make breaks in proportion to the rest of the
      drawing (specifically in drawings which have been scaled) by setting
      Dimscale to the desired scale factor and replacing the line: (setq cc 2)
      with: (setq cc (* 2 (getvar "dimscale")))
      The 2 can in either case be replaced with your favourite break width.
      See also JACK.LSP which inserts a block at Dimscale.

SIX.LSP
Lottery ticket number generator (for the 649 format).  For reasons
presumably having to do with the internal workings of Intel's chips,
running this several times in succession will often produce similar
though not identical numbers.  So run it only once at a time, and content
yourself with the thought that randomness lies not in uniqueness but in
lack of predictability.  And remember me if you win.

SL.LSP
Erase the second last entity created.  Surprisingly useful, but only if it
is quicker than just hitting Erase and the offending entity; it basically
has to be on the digitizer menu.  See various notes herein on the
desirability of customizing AutoCAD.

SLYNX.LSP
The original version of Lynx which adjusts lines to the current snap unit
(if snap is on) and places the resulting line on the current layer.
See Lynx.lsp.

SNAKE.LSP (New)
Similar to Snapper, but with one difference: this one not only moves the
insertion point of entities to the nearest snap point, it alters their
dimensions to make them fit.  Both ends of a line are put on snap, as are
both endpoints of fitted or aligned text and all vertices of a polyline; the
centre of a circle is snapped and the radius is altered to the nearest
multiple of the current snap, all four corners of a solid or trace are put
on snap.  Arcs, point entities, block insertions and centre, middle, right
and left justified text entities are moved unaltered to the nearest snap
point.  Unlike Snapper, Snake will usually leave corners of rectangles etc.
connected even if it alters their shape.  Despite this it is best used with
a degree of caution, especially on polylines such as polygons, doughnuts and
ellipses.

SNAME.LSP
Prints the last eight digits of the drawing name in the upper right hand
corner of the screen (that is, at the top of the screen menu area where
it usually says AutoCAD).  Since any program containing the (grtext)
command will return things to normal (as will in most cases a shell) this
is generally intended to be used as (S::STARTUP) in ACAD.LSP as a quick check
on whether you have entered the correct drawing or built into some other
command which is used fairly often if you wish the name to be continuously
displayed (it is built into TXTT.LSP).  I'm not sure why Autocad doesn't
do this automatically - while it's nice to see the program name displayed
at all times I forget which drawing I called up a lot more often than I
forget that I'm in AutoCAD.

SNAPPER.LSP
Puts entities (back...) on snap, by moving their insertion point to the
nearest multiple of Snapunit.  Observe that this does not change entities,
just moves them.  Also, it will ignore polylines (including donuts and
ellipses) since putting each vertex on snap would most likely produce an
entity having little resemblance to the original, besides which the time
required to write such an ability into the program is not offset by the
minimal likelyhood of anybody ever requiring it.  Arcs are moved so that
their centre point is on snap; traces are moved but bear in mind that each
segment is a separate entity and will be moved as such.  As far as the
complex 3D entities go, you're on your own.  I would recommend that you save
the drawing before trying anything rash.  See also Snake.lsp.

SPARE.LSP
Space remover.  Eradicates those free-floating spaces which mysteriously
show up on the ends of text strings.  If you use one of the VB's and the
text seems not to be correctly positioned as a result then you probably
should try this.

SPRAY.LSP (New)
This program takes text from a file, from an existing text entity, or from
the keyboard (if you can type) and places it at intervals along an existing
line, circle, arc or polyline; if none of these suit you it will construct a
circle of appropriate size.  Text can be placed in a variety of ways
depending on the entity used as a base: clockwise or counterclockwise around
a circle or arc, away from or towards the origin of a polyline, and upside
down or upright on a line.
If the source is a file Spray can extract one line (incrementing the line
number after each use) or use the entire thing, but bear in mind that a file
will crash Spray if it's too long for the available memory - specifically
the heap space, which depends on your machine and settings.  This shouldn't
be a problem if you use this feature as a way of preparing text before
applying it to an entity rather than trying to plaster Command.com onto
three hundred foot circles.  (Later note: a way around this just occurred to
me but it'll have to wait for the next revision.  If you really need it,
call me.)  There are other prompts and options but they are better tried out
than explained.  (I try to write programs so that the defaults will produce
some kind of result even if the user has no idea what the routine does.
There may be those who would argue that if one doesn't know what a program
does it may not be a wise idea to run it, or that this approach contributes
to software piracy, but I treat them with the indifference they deserve.)
Spray works well with LTR.LSP - you can write a text file with LTR (or with
a word processor if you want to use conventional methods) and wrap it around
various entities, or dump the whole file into a drawing with Import.lsp
(which is included with AutoCAD) and then suck selected lines onto nearby
entities with Spray.
Your employer is probably not going to ditch his word processing package for
this method even when you point out that AutoCAD comes a lot closer than the
majority of word processors to what has come to be known as WYSIWYG. (By the
way, with the exception of the military, who make up strings of initials
with the express intention of using them as words, only small children and
cretins try to pronounce acronyms.)  Still, Spray can dress up your less
serious correspondence and may even have some use of which your company
would approve, if you're concerned about that sort of thing.  It was, I seem
to recall, created for a purpose but history tends to obscure that sort
of thing if the program takes more than a day or two to write...
Spray requires the blocks CW.DWG and CCW.DWG which are included.  Each
consists of a letter "X", which Spray inserts and then changes to the
required letter.  If you want to change the style, colour, height, etc. of
the text you can either change the finished product using one of the variety
of lisps in this package or edit the blocks.  (The blocks use a text height
of six drawing units; this will probably not be appropriate if a unit
represents something other than a millimetre.)
Bear in mind that Autocad drawings can be imported into Word Perfect using a
variety of methods.  (See Bill Fane's excellent article in the March 1990
Cadalyst.)

SPYR.LSP (New)
A fascinating routine which came into being by accident while I was
attempting to write PLANT.  Spyr prompts for a start point, segment length,
start angle, angle increment, length multiplier, and number of segments.
Then it draws lines, incrementing the angle and length each time.  The
result is one of a variety of patterns which are much more interesting to
see than to read about.  The increments (which are the heart of the matter)
are used as defaults the next time around in case you want to do the same
thing again or try some close variation.
I recently occupied a few idle minutes by making a number of spirals with
Spyr, making slides of them (with the Mslide command, in case you can't be
bothered to look it up), and writing (with BATCH.LSP) a script file to
display the slides in a repeating loop to the amazement of my colleagues,
none of whom have ever read the manual.  The general format of the script
file, in case you are interested, is:
vslide slidename
delay 1000
vslide anotherslidename
delay 1000
RSCRIPT
(The delays are optional.)

SQ.LSP
Sequential text changer.  Select text entities, each one will be highlighted
in turn at which point you can type in a replacement string or <Return>
to leave the existing one unchanged.  As usual, ignores entities other
than text.  Probably the routine I use most often.  (And TCH.LSP)

ST.LSP
Short text style changer.  No screen menu, but: 1) it won't crash lightning
zoom and 2) it allows example selection instead.  (See SC.LSP)

STAR.LSP (New)
This routine suggested itself after I stumbled into SPYR.LSP.  It displays a
slide file (default "Star" or the most recent one used) and selects the
entire drawing.  This leaves you with the drawing you were working on
superimposed on the background of your choice.  It has been suggested that
one can copy drawings without importing them by making a slide of the
original, running Star, and then tracing over the desired portion, an
interesting idea if not one for which uses spring unbidden to the mind like
gazelles.  The original inspiration was purely aesthetic, though.  Bear in
mind that a REDRAW, PAN or ZOOM will erase the slide.

SWX.LSP
Switch positions of two groups of entities by selecting one group, a
basepoint, the second group, and another basepoint.  Easier than moving
one group out of the way, putting the second group in its place, and moving
the first group to where the second one used to be.
Generally this one doesn't get rave reviews - largely I think because it
seems to require a brain to use.  Actually a modicum of common sense will
suffice, if you have any this is worth at least a try: I use it all the time.

TACH.LSP
Suck a text string out of a text entity and inject it into an attribute.

TB.LSP
Terminal block drawer.  Draws a series of connected boxes, each with a text
line, you specify text height, box height, box width, starting number,
number of terminals and of course the ubiquitous "Start point:".  This will
not handle real numbers (the ones with decimal places) but negatives are OK
if your terminal blocks are peculiarly named.
See TBTX.LSP below.

TBTX.LSP (New)
A slightly more elaborate version of TB.LSP, this allows text before and
after the incremented number, and prompts for the angle at which to draw the
block.  I'm not too sure about the ability to draw angled terminal blocks
but the text feature seems useful; anyway both modifications were requested
by someone who was really using the thing which is a fairly forceful argument.
I have left the original TB in for those uses which don't require text or
angles since, as mentioned elsewhere, I think that a program should have as
few prompts as is practical.  (I would even recommend that if you find a
certain prompt superfluous you edit the routine either to make the most
common value the default or to remove the prompt entirely.)

TCH.LSP
Change one or more lines of text to a single text string which is either
typed in or copied from an existing text string by picking it.  One of the
few truly indispensable Lisps.
A variety of programs to mash most qualities of text (or other entities)
can be based on this structure.  (You can use GET.LSP to find out just what
part of the association list you need to alter.)

TCHS.LSP
Single text changer.  Written (really) because certain parties object to the
extra <Return> needed to tell TCH that the selection set is complete.
At the same time it will repeat at a <Return> and thus is superior (for that
purpose) to the Change command or any menu driven work-around.

TOGGLE.LSP (New)
A simple device for toggling system variables between 0 and 1; intended
for incorporation into menus.  You can either call Toggle or just use a
menu line based on the form:
[Toggle Highlight](setvar "Highlight" (abs (1- (getvar "Highlight"))))
This method doesn't tell you what it just did, but it is transparent,
which can be useful if changes to the variable take effect before the
command is finished (which is not the case with highlight).

TTC.LSP
This and the next five listings - TTF, TTL, TTM, and TTR - will alter the
registration type of a line of text.  Actually what they do is extract the
text string from the entity data, erase the original entity, and construct a
new one of the desired registration with its insertion on the specified
point.  If the current text style, colour, width scale factor, rotation or
layer are not the same as those of the original entity then the newly
created text line will also differ in the appropriate respect, this can be
useful if you are trying to standardise a number of text entities but if you
wish it is relatively simple to modify a program so that it either changes
the characteristic in question, refuses to touch an entity in which it
differs from the current value, or prompts before making the change.
(VBHX.LSP, which checks to see that the current text style is the same as
that of a selected entity before attacking it, is included as an example.)
(Note: see also Align.lsp, Centre.lsp, Fit.lsp, Left.lsp, Middle.lsp and
Right.lsp which change the existing entity rather than constructing a new
one and thus leave style, layer etc. unaltered.)

A quick aside:
As I may have mentioned before, the use of fixed height text is analogous
to the use of a diesel locomotive on the highway - the lack of steering
will save a lot of time which might be wasted on decisions concerning
trajectory.  These routines will handle fixed height text, but if the set
height is not the same as that of the text being rejustified, things may
change a little more than you wanted.  This is not a problem which arises
with variable height text styles.  Just in case there is someone reading
this who doesn't know what I am ranting about: when one switches text styles
Autocad prompts for a variety of things, among them text height.  If you
reply for instance "3" then the text height will be set at three drawing
units, you will not be prompted for a height when writing text, and Change
will not offer you the option of specifying a new height.  If on the other
hand you respond "0" then you will be asked for a height when writing a
line of text, you will be able to change it with Change, your life will
be easier and more rational and your co-workers will respect you and maybe
even voice the misguided theory that you know what you are doing.  If you
hate those few extra keystrokes enough to actually do something about them,
then add an "Insert 2.5mm text" option to your menu.  Mine has text options
in the standard heights for right, left, centred, and fitted text, not to
mention the invaluable HT.LSP.
Modifying the menu is not as complicated as it appears, by the way.  A chimp
could do it.  Maybe even your boss.  You can even shell out of Autocad and
make changes on the fly.  (Keep a backup copy of the original in case the
new version doesn't work as planned, just to save yourself from panic until
you can fix it.)
The Autocad manual - which despite a fair amount of unwarranted criticism
from a variety of sources is well laid out and almost invariably clearly
written - has a section on menu modification which has everything you really
need to know.  Modifying the pulldowns is a good place to start: you don't
have to replot the tablet overlay, and mistakes usually only affect the
pulldown you altered.  Anyway, to return to the subject at hand, the other
four TT's:

TTF.LSP - Text to fit.  (My God!  The version of this in the last release
                         didn't work.  This one does.  Sorry about that.)
TTL.LSP - Text to left.

TTM.LSP - Text to middle.

TTR.LSP - Text to right.


TXD.LSP
Text to date.  Pick a line of text, this will change it to the date - the
long format: JULY 12, 1969.  If you wish to use abbreviations you can edit
the Lisp file.  For that matter you can make up your own month names if you
are so inclined.
This file contains the lines:     (if (= (substr (cdr zz) 1 4) "ASB:")
                                      (setq dd (strcat "ASB: " dd)))

They check whether the original text started with "ASB:" and if so add
it to the start of the new string.  This can be edited to find any desired
string and add it or another to the new text.  (The number 4 in the first
line must be changed to the length of the string to be found.)  Alternately
the second line can be used alone to unconditionally add any desired string.
You will have to remove the last ")".

TXMD.LSP
Text to metric date.  Similar to the above, but in the DD/MM/YY format.
I'm not sure what is metric about this since it's not in kiloseconds or
deciyears, the powers that be haven't threatened to imprison people who
won't use it, and it isn't common knowledge that if we all believed in it
wholeheartedly we could be just like the Japanese.  Nonetheless, that's what
it's called, and here it is.

TXKILL.LSP (New)
Similar to Pkill, this finds empty text strings ("" or " ") which according
to rumour can cause trouble with Zoom Extents.  If they are the problem, if
you begrudge the small amount of memory they consume, or if they just
irritate you Txkill can eradicate them.  It prompts first just in case you
want them for some reason...
See also Sdel.lsp and Pkill.lsp.

TXN.LSP
Text to title without path.  The title will be as you entered it to call
up the drawing.  This is not a process which allows much room for creativity,
but if you want the result to be capitalised you have to enter it that way.
(Or use UC.LSP, or modify this to do it automatically.)  See also TXTT.LSP
and QUONSET.LSP.

TXS.LSP
Global text size changer - you specify initial and desired sizes, and
anything in the drawing of the initial size will be changed to the desired
one.  There are a couple of these floating around, and while I would like
to be able to say that everything in here was mystically inspired by a
source of ideas that only I have access to, it was actually suggested by
MI, who has an amazing talent for ferreting out half-baked lisps from
unlikely sources and who was dissatisfied with a similar routine which
changed every text line in a drawing to one height (a useful ability if
your output device is a daisy wheel printer).
If you want to edit out the "Eat leaden death" effect (while we're on
the subject of half-bakedness) just take out the (grclear) line.  It is
harmless, though: a redraw will restore things.

TXTT.LSP
Text to title with path.  Same idea as TXN.LSP and QUONSET.LSP.

TXX.LSP
Very short SQ - sequential text changer.  Included in case you want something
a bit faster and easier on memory and without the debriefing at the end.

UC.LSP
Convert text to upper case.  Similar to LCS.LSP.

VB.LSP
Venetian blind - multiple text rejustify to fit.  Most of the comments for
the TT?.LSP routines apply here.  This takes a number of lines of text and
fits them between two specified points while keeping the original vertical
placement in the drawing.  The VB's are very nice for cleaning up sloppy
columns of text, especially since they change everything to the current
height and style.  I once had occasion to work on a set of drawings done
by a party who did not use snap, considered all text styles to be identical,
and who liked to mix variable and fixed height text.  VBC enabled me to
clean the average drawing up in about five minutes, which impressed both
the boss and my fellow employees who had refused to have anything to do
with the situation.  (An unretouched testimonial by an innocent party.)
Another trick worth keeping in mind if you're bored: Type VB, window the
whole drawing and then watch all of the text suck into a single column.
Then tell your supervisor it just happened by itself.
The variants:

VBC.LSP - Venetian blind centred.  This one allows you to pick two points
and centres the text on a point halfway between them.  Designed this way
because it largely gets used to centre text in boxes.  If you only want to
specify one point, pick twice on the same spot.  Or edit the routine.
(If you are only dealing with one entity, by the way, TTM.LSP will centre
text horizontally and vertically: pick the points on diagonally opposite
corners of the box in question.)

VBHX.LSP - Venetian blind centred; ignores text if the current text style
is not the same as that of the text entity.  See TTC.LSP for an explanation
of the need for this general form.
The other VB's:

VBL.LSP - Venetian blind left justified.

VBM.LSP - Venetian blind middle justified.

VBR.LSP - Venetian blind right justified.

VVB.LSP
Vertical venetian blind.  Takes a column of text and respaces it vertically.
You specify the start point and the vertical spacing.  Note that this does
not move text horizontally, only vertically.

WMX.LSP
Move a word to the next line, or the previous one, or to a new line.
This program occurred to me after working with several connected lines
of text and realizing that text handling was not Autocad's strongest point.
(It still isn't.)  It emulates one of the most useful features of a word
processor - it can remove a word from the end of one line and move it to
another.  If the first line selected is above the second then the last word
in the upper line is moved to the beginning of the next.  If the first line
is below the second then the action is exactly reversed.  If no second line
is selected then a new one is constructed and the user is prompted for a
start point.  Spaces are inserted automatically.
Notes: 1.  If you just want to join two lines of text together see LADD.LSP.
       2.  Since WMX uses the Subst and Entmod functions it can move a word
           from one line to another in a different style with no trouble.
       3.  Wmx can also suck up an individual word and put it on the end of
           a line of text.
       4.  Where does a word begin or end?  At a space, so you can't chop
           text at a dash or ampersand (unless you modify the routine).
This, SQ, TCH and the indispensable Chgtext enable one to perform most of
the more basic functions of a word processor while in the drawing editor.
(See also SC.LSP.)  If that's not enough check out ED.LSP.

WT.LSP
Multiple width scale factor change.  Similar to HT.LSP but changes width.
Mostly useless, occasionally invaluable.

XING.LSP
Crossing locator and breaker.  Select a number of lines.  This will find the
intersections between them (if any) and put a two unit break in the vertical
line of each crossing unless it is an endpoint in which case it will be
untouched.  Not only is this much faster than doing it by hand (it prints
the elapsed time in case you are in doubt), it is more uniform than your
co-workers who think that snap is in infringement on their freedom of
expression (Just in case you agree with this odd viewpoint, a wrench is
an infringement on your freedom to remove spark plugs with your bare hands.
Need I continue?) and is much more interesting to watch (unless they are
strikingly good looking or belong to a cult which requires the wearing of
a gorilla suit at all times).
It can also be modified - for instance to insert a block or other entity
at line intersections, not to ignore endpoints, to break the horizontal
line or to use a wider perforation (see note 3).
Cautionary notes:
       1.  Since the Break command requires a pick rather than an entity
          name, you must be zoomed in closely enough that Autocad won't hit
          the wrong line at the intersection.  In practice that isn't that
          close, a little experimentation will demonstrate rough limits.
       2.  If your lines aren't on snap then they may either overlap slightly
          in which case endpoints will be treated as intersections and cut,
          or they may not meet in which case there will be no intersection
          and nothing will happen.  You just can't place lines to sixteen
          decimal places by eye.
       3.  If you are working in inches a two unit gap may be too large.  If
          you are are doing an 8.5 X 11 drawing of an object that's sixty
          feet long a two inch gap may be two small.  If your line is the
          same length as the gap it will be totally obliterated, and if it's
          a lot shorter then Break won't be able to find it and XING will
          crash.  (This won't damage your drawing but it is irritating.)
These make it seem that XING is a command that is never likely to work.
On the contrary, it is a very useful and reliable routine and only modesty
prevents my running on at some length about it.
See also SING.LSP.

XX.LSP
Shells out and fires up a word processor with the file specified by the ff
default.  (See LD.LSP) If no extension is specified it adds ".lsp".  Very
useful if you are editing lisp files or repeatedly editing any text file
from within Autocad.  (Why XX?  Because it's easy to find on the keyboard.)
The line: (command "xxx" fn) uses the command "xxx" to shell out to a text
editor.  As with ED.LSP you will have to add the line:
XXX,editor,200000,File to edit: ,4
to your Acad.pgp file, where "editor" is the name of a text editor.
If you are not familiar with the shell command file Acad.pgp see Sample.pgp.

ZA.LSP
Zoom All with timer.  A companion to RA.LSP and ZP.LSP.  This might save you
from going berserk with boredom, but actually a little constructive idleness
is safer.  Or writing some Lisp.  Or going for coffee in another city.

ZE.LSP
Zoom Extents by doing a Zoom Window using the Extmin and Extmax system
variables as the corners.  Saves doing Zoom previous six times, or doing
a Zoom All if your computer has forgotten the first three zooms you did.
If you have not forced a regen zooming inward, this shouldn't force one
going out (and if it does you would have had to do one anyway).  Also a
Zoom Previous will undo the effect of this, whereas it won't undo the
effect of another Zoom Previous.  (See also ZVS.LSP)

ZP.LSP
Zoom Previous with timer.  See also ZA.LSP and ZP.LSP.  The original version
of this had a line: (write-line "Command: Zoom")
and another: (write-line "Zoom: All/Nothing/Hi mom/Infinite/Oblivion/(x): P")
or whatever the prompt is so as to give the impression that I had actually
entered the proper command, my superiors taking a dim view of Lisp for some
reason not clear to me (or, I imagine, to them).  You can insert some version
of them before the line (command "zoom" "p") if you're bored or paranoid.

ZVS.LSP
Zoom to the virtual screen limits with no regen - i.e.  zoom out as far as
possible on a redraw.  Doesn't always zoom out as far (or as accurately)
as ZE.LSP but then also doesn't risk a regen.